.include {docs}

# go2_preprocessor

`go2_preprocessor` turns the raw Go2 lidar cloud and body IMU into what Point-LIO expects: both in
the body frame, corrected with the numbers from `go2_calibration`.

**What.** One ROS 2 node (`ros2 run go2_preprocessor go2_preprocessor`). It runs while you map. It
listens to the raw lidar cloud and to the robot state, fixes both with the calibration file, and
publishes two clean topics for Point-LIO:

- the cloud, moved into the body frame, with the ranges shortened and the robot's own points removed;
- the body IMU, with the gyroscope bias removed and the accelerometer scaled to 9.81.

**Why.** Point-LIO builds the map from a cloud and an IMU. They must share one frame and tell the
truth. The raw Go2 data does neither:

- the cloud is in the lidar frame, and the lidar is mounted upside down and tilted;
- every lidar range reads about 0.11 m too long;
- the body IMU has a bias, and a still accelerometer reads 9.44 m/s^2 instead of 9.81;
- the lidar sees the robot's own back.

Fed raw, Point-LIO draws a tilted and stretched map.

**When.** Every time you map. It is the first node of the mapping chain, and `mapping.launch.py`
starts it.

## Where it sits

.mermaid
    flowchart TB
      subgraph robot["Go2 topics"]
        cloud["/utlidar/cloud"]
        state["/sportmodestate"]
      end
      robot --> pre[go2_preprocessor]
      cal[(go2_calibration.yaml)] --> pre
      box[(preprocessor.yaml)] --> pre
      launch["mapping.launch.py"] -.->|starts| pre
      pre -->|"/utlidar/transformed_cloud"| lio[Point-LIO]
      pre -->|"/utlidar/transformed_imu"| lio

The measured numbers (`lidar_to_body`, `body_imu`, `range_offset`) come from the
[go2_calibration](./go2_calibration.qd) file. The robot box is written by hand in `preprocessor.yaml`.
The lidar's own IMU (`/utlidar/imu`) is not used: its accelerometer is synthetic.

## What it changes

| Data | What the node does | Parameter |
|---|---|---|
| Cloud, every range | Moves each point back towards the lidar. A point closer than the offset is dropped | `range_offset` |
| Cloud, every point | Turns it from the lidar frame into the body frame: `p_body = R * p_lidar + t` | `lidar_to_body.*` |
| Cloud, robot's own points | Drops the points inside a box around the robot's back (body frame) | `robot_box.*` |
| IMU, gyroscope | Subtracts the still bias | `body_imu.gyro_bias.*` |
| IMU, accelerometer | Multiplies by the scale that makes a still reading 9.81 | `body_imu.accel_scale` |
| IMU, repeated samples | Drops a sample whose stamp is not newer than the last one | none |

Intensity, ring and time of each point, and the stamp of the cloud, pass through unchanged.

## Quick start

With the whole mapping chain (preprocessor, Point-LIO, map builder):

```bash
ros2 launch go2_mapping_bringup mapping.launch.py calibration_file:=$HOME/calibration/current.yaml
```

`tools/mapping/map.sh` starts the same chain with the current calibration. The node alone:

```bash
ros2 run go2_preprocessor go2_preprocessor --ros-args \
    --params-file src/go2_mapping_bringup/config/preprocessor.yaml \
    --params-file $HOME/calibration/current.yaml
```

The node name must stay `go2_preprocessor`: both files are keyed by it, and a different name makes
ROS ignore them without an error.

## How it works

### The code in one picture

Everything is one executable. The files in the box are plain C++ with Eigen (a matrix library),
except the node, which needs ROS. An arrow means "uses".

.mermaid
    flowchart TB
      subgraph EXE["one executable: go2_preprocessor"]
        main["main: starts the node"]
        node["preprocessor_node: PreprocessorNode"]
        conv["convertor: from_message, to_message"]
        rob["robot_utils: shorten_ranges, lidar_to_body, remove_robot_points, preprocess_cloud"]
        imu["imu_transform: calibrate_gyro, calibrate_accel"]
        cal["calibration: rotation_from_parameter, vector_from_parameter"]
        sch["schemas: Point, PointCloud, Box"]
      end
      main --> node
      node --> conv
      node --> rob
      node --> imu
      node --> cal
      conv --> sch
      rob --> sch

### Start-up

The node reads every setting once, when it starts. All of them are read-only parameters: a later
`ros2 param set` fails with an error instead of being silently ignored.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
      participant L as launch file
      participant R as ROS parameters
      participant N as PreprocessorNode
      participant C as calibration.cpp
      L->>R: preprocessor.yaml, then calibration_file
      N->>R: declare robot_box.* (6 values)
      N->>R: declare lidar_to_body.rotation (9) and translation (3)
      N->>C: rotation_from_parameter(9 values)
      C-->>N: a rotation matrix, or an error
      N->>R: declare body_imu.* and range_offset
      N->>N: log the calibration, one line per value
      N->>N: create 2 subscriptions and 2 publishers

`rotation_from_parameter` refuses a matrix that is not a rotation (stretched axes, or a mirror image),
so a typo in the file stops the node at start-up. The log line `calibration:` shows whether the
file was applied: look for your numbers there.

### A cloud message

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
      participant G as Go2
      participant N as on_cloud
      participant V as convertor
      participant U as robot_utils
      participant P as Point-LIO
      G->>N: /utlidar/cloud
      N->>V: from_message(message)
      V-->>N: PointCloud, lidar frame
      N->>U: preprocess_cloud(cloud, lidar_to_body, robot_box, range_offset)
      U->>U: 1 shorten_ranges
      U->>U: 2 lidar_to_body
      U->>U: 3 remove_robot_points
      U-->>N: PointCloud, body frame
      N->>V: to_message(cloud)
      N->>P: /utlidar/transformed_cloud

The order matters. The robot box is written in the body frame, so the points are turned **before**
the box is applied. The ranges are shortened first because the offset belongs to the lidar: it is
measured along each ray from the lidar's centre. A cloud that cannot be read (data shorter than its
declared size, or a missing field) is logged and skipped.

**Example.** Real run of the actual code, with the calibration of 2026-10-19 (`lidar_to_body` and
`range_offset` 0.1129 m). The points are made up: they were computed back from a known place in the
body frame, with every range 0.1129 m too long, the way the lidar reads them.

| Point | True place in the body frame | What the lidar reads (lidar frame) | Without `range_offset` | With `range_offset` 0.1129 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| wall | (2.000, 0.500, -0.100) | (0.500, -1.746, 0.575), 1.905 m | (2.108, 0.532, -0.106) | (2.000, 0.500, -0.100) |
| floor | (0.800, -0.200, -0.320) | (0.465, -0.284, 0.521), 0.754 m | (0.891, -0.235, -0.376) | (0.800, -0.200, -0.320) |
| robot's back | (-0.400, 0.000, -0.250) | (-0.455, 0.701, 0.075), 0.839 m | dropped, inside the box | dropped, inside the box |

Without the correction the wall sits 11 cm too far and the floor 5.6 cm too low.

### An IMU message

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
      m["/sportmodestate message"] --> s{"stamp newer than the last one?"}
      s -->|no| drop(["dropped: a repeated sample"])
      s -->|yes| g["gyro = gyroscope - gyro_bias"]
      g --> a["accel = accelerometer * accel_scale"]
      a --> h["Imu message: stamp of the message, frame body"]
      h --> p(["publish /utlidar/transformed_imu"])

1 to 1.5 % of the messages repeat the previous sample, with the same stamp and the same values. Point-LIO
would take the copy as one more measurement at the same instant, so the node drops it.

The stamp is the one **inside** the message, which uses the lidar's clock, and not the receive time.
The orientation and the covariances stay at their defaults ("unknown"): Point-LIO reads only the
rates and the acceleration.

**Example.** The body IMU at rest on our Go2: the accelerometer reads (-0.146, -0.133, 9.444), with
length 9.446. The scale `9.81 / 9.446` = 1.0385 turns it into (-0.152, -0.138, 9.808). The small sideways
parts grow by the same factor, because it is a scale and not a bias.

### Types

.mermaid
    classDiagram
      class PreprocessorNode
      class SensorCalibration
      class BodyImuCalibration
      class Box
      class PointCloud
      class Point
      PreprocessorNode *-- SensorCalibration : calibration_
      PreprocessorNode *-- Box : robot_body_
      SensorCalibration *-- BodyImuCalibration : body_imu
      PointCloud *-- Point : points

| File | Does |
|---|---|
| `main` | Starts the node and spins |
| `preprocessor_node` | The two callbacks, the parameters and the topics |
| `convertor` | `PointCloud2` message to `PointCloud` and back |
| `robot_utils` | `shorten_ranges`, `lidar_to_body`, `remove_robot_points`, `preprocess_cloud` |
| `imu_transform` | `calibrate_gyro` and `calibrate_accel` |
| `calibration` | Turns the parameter numbers into a rotation and a vector, and checks them |
| `schemas` | `Point`, `PointCloud` and `Box` (`contains` uses strict limits) |

## Parameters

All are read-only ROS parameters, read once at start-up.

| Parameter | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `lidar_to_body.rotation` | CMU's mount | 9 numbers, row-major. Must be a rotation |
| `lidar_to_body.translation` | (0, 0, -0.046825) | m, 3 numbers |
| `body_imu.gyro_bias.x`, `.y`, `.z` | 0 | rad/s, subtracted from the gyroscope |
| `body_imu.accel_scale` | 1.0 | multiplies the accelerometer |
| `range_offset` | 0 | m, subtracted from every lidar range |
| `robot_box.min_x`, `max_x` | -0.7, -0.1 | m, body frame |
| `robot_box.min_y`, `max_y` | -0.3, 0.3 | m |
| `robot_box.min_z`, `max_z` | -0.646825, -0.046825 | m |

A name that is not in the table is not an error: ROS ignores it, and the default is used. Check the
`calibration:` log line.

## Topics

| Direction | Topic | Type | Queue |
|---|---|---|---|
| in | `/utlidar/cloud` | `sensor_msgs/PointCloud2` | sensor data QoS |
| in | `/sportmodestate` | `unitree_go/SportModeState` | sensor data QoS |
| out | `/utlidar/transformed_cloud` | `sensor_msgs/PointCloud2`, frame `body` | 10 |
| out | `/utlidar/transformed_imu` | `sensor_msgs/Imu`, frame `body` | 50 |

The topic names are fixed in the code. The output IMU queue is deeper because the IMU is much
faster than the lidar.

## Build and test

Needs Unitree's `unitree_go` messages. On the robot they come with `~/unitree_ros2`:

```bash
cd ~/unitree_go2
source ~/unitree_ros2/setup.sh
colcon build --packages-select go2_preprocessor
colcon test --packages-select go2_preprocessor && colcon test-result --verbose
```

Four test programs, one per file: `test_convertor` (round trip and layout), `test_schemas` (the box
limits), `test_robot_utils` (turn, filter, and both in order), `test_imu_transform`. Each one
compiles the file it tests again, because the code lives in the executable and not in a library.

## Known gaps

- **The box is still CMU's.** `robot_box` was made for CMU's `lidar_to_body` translation
  (0, 0, -0.046825). Ours is (0.282, 0, 0). The comment in `preprocessor.yaml` says to check it in
  RViz with the real calibration. Not done yet.
- **No calibration file means CMU's mount.** Started alone without a calibration file, the node uses
  CMU's rotation and no IMU correction, and the map comes out tilted. `mapping.launch.py` requires
  `calibration_file`, but `ros2 run` does not. Only the start-up log shows it.
- **`calibration.cpp` has no unit test.** It is what stops a stretched or mirrored rotation, and
  the wrong number of values. The four test programs do not include it. `shorten_ranges` has none
  yet either.
- **The log line counts range drops as robot points.** `robot points removed` is points in minus
  points out, so it also counts the points dropped for being closer than `range_offset`.
